concepts.body:
**Enums** (PHP 8.1) define a type with a fixed set of cases. A pure enum has only names; a **backed** enum maps each case to an `int` or `string`, which is what you store in a database or JSON:

```php
enum Status: string {
    case Draft = 'draft';
    case Published = 'published';
}
```

`Status::from('draft')` converts a value to a case and throws a `ValueError` for unknown values; `Status::tryFrom('x')` returns `null` instead. `Status::cases()` lists all cases, and `$case->value` and `$case->name` read them. Cases are singletons, so compare with `===`. Enums can have methods, constants and implement interfaces, which pairs nicely with `match`. They cannot hold mutable state.

**Readonly properties** (8.1) can be assigned once, from inside the class, and never changed. A **readonly class** (8.2) makes every declared property readonly. Together with constructor promotion they make compact **value objects**:

```php
final readonly class Money {
    public function __construct(public int $cents, public string $currency) {}
}
```

To "change" an immutable object, return a new one from a `with...()` method. Since PHP 8.3 readonly properties can be reinitialised inside `__clone`, which makes clone-based withers possible.

Readonly is shallow: a readonly property holding an object prevents replacing the object, not mutating it.

