concepts.body:
A function containing `yield` becomes a **generator**: calling it returns a `Generator` object and runs nothing yet. Each `foreach` step resumes the function until the next `yield`, receives that value, and pauses again.

```php
function lines(string $path): Generator {
    $h = fopen($path, 'r');
    try {
        while (($line = fgets($h)) !== false) yield rtrim($line, "\n");
    } finally { fclose($h); }
}
```

This reads a 10 GB file with constant memory, because only one line exists at a time. Generators compose: one generator can filter or map another, building lazy pipelines.

- `yield $key => $value` yields keys too.
- `yield from $other` delegates to another generator or array.
- A generator can `return` a final value, read with `$gen->getReturn()` after it finishes.
- `$gen->send($x)` resumes it and makes the paused `yield` expression evaluate to `$x`, enabling two-way coroutines.

A generator runs once; it can't be rewound after it has started. To make an object iterable many times, implement `IteratorAggregate` and return a fresh generator from `getIterator()`.

Watch out with `iterator_to_array($gen)`: it uses keys by default, and `yield from` restarts keys at 0 for each inner source, so values overwrite each other. Pass `preserve_keys: false`.

A **Fiber** (PHP 8.1) is a function with its own call stack that can pause itself anywhere and be resumed later. Unlike a generator, it can suspend from deep inside nested calls, and the functions it calls need no special syntax.

```php
$fiber = new Fiber(function (string $x): string {
    $y = Fiber::suspend($x . '!');   // pause, handing a value out
    return "got $y";
});
$out = $fiber->start('hi');   // runs until suspend: 'hi!'
$fiber->resume('data');       // suspend() returns 'data'
$fiber->getReturn();          // 'got data'
```

Fibers are cooperative and single-threaded. Only one runs at a time, and a fiber pauses only when it calls `Fiber::suspend()`. They give no parallelism for CPU work.

What they enable is **async I/O**. An event loop (the Revolt loop used by AMPHP v3 and ReactPHP's async package) starts many fibers. When one would block on a socket, the library suspends that fiber and registers interest in the socket; when data arrives, the loop resumes it. Application code just writes `$response = $client->request($url);`, while dozens of requests are in flight concurrently.

Most applications never touch Fiber directly; they use these libraries. Remember the constraints: blocking functions such as `sleep()`, `file_get_contents()` on URLs or PDO queries still block the whole process, so you need async-aware drivers, and state shared between fibers needs care just like in any concurrent program.
